This code is a very basic example and may not handle some specifics cases perfectly. More complex cases, like handling properties with different accessor types, handling generic methods, and more complex asserts needs a full parser or syntax tokens.

Please note that C# syntax has multiple parts and it's hard to parse all of them in one simple function. For example, it handles multiple asserts per method, `static` accessor, and multiple types of return statements (`return` and `throw`). It's best to use the C# Roslyn compiler, if the scenario is complex and needs full-fledged C# parsing.
Also, please keep in mind that C# code parsing logic can change with changes in C# language specification. It is strongly recommended to use stable and officially-supported parsers or syntax tokens (like `CSharpSyntaxTree` from Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp in .NET 5 or later).--------------------
