<code>
Debug.Assert(Array.IndexOf(array, value, 0, _size) == Array.LastIndexOf(array, value, _size - 1, _size));
</code>

Each of these assert statements must be checked using `Debug.Assert` to avoid runtime exceptions. The corresponding assertion checks whether the state of a collection before and after the operation remain consistent, such as the count of elements or changes to the last element, or if an element can be found within the collection both from the start and end sides. The OLD(var) syntax is used to store the previous state of the variable being tested in cases where testing needs to be done over several operations. The `LastIndexOf` function is used to compare if the correct index is returned for both start and end side operations.

Please replace `ArrayList` with the name of the list variable you used in your C# code and also replace `array` and `value` with the name of your array and the value you are searching for in the `Array.IndexOf` or `Array.LastIndexOf` call.

Note: This solution assumes you have access to the original list to re-apply the operation before the assertion. It would be very difficult to create an assertion that tests a function directly from the function definition. Thus you need to run the function and record the initial state of the list, and then ensure the list is reset to its initial state before running the next assertion.--------------------
Debug.Assert(OLD(_size) - 1 == RET);
Debug.Assert(OLD(_size) - 1 == Array.LastIndexOf((Array)_items, value, 0, _size));
<code>--------------------
Debug.Assert(OLD(x) + y == x);
</code>
In this case the variable `x` is not declared before the operation so the `OLD(variable_name)` is not applicable. However, we can infer that the operation performed is addition where `x` increases by `y`. Hence, the assertion would be `Debug.Assert(x + y == old_x)`.

The assertion for the comment that starts with "@@@" is "if the array contains x before the operation, the first index of x decreases or remains same after the operation".
In this case, we might infer that the ArrayList's Remove() method is being used, and the first index of the removed element decreases or remains the same. The assertion here would be `Debug.Assert(Array.IndexOf(list, x) < OLD(Array.IndexOf(list, x)))`.
Let's use indexOf function for ArrayList, it returns -1 when an array does not contain a certain element. Hence, the assertion would be "Debug.Assert(Array.IndexOf list, oldx - (oldx - x) == Array.IndexOf list, x)".--------------------
Debug.Assert(Old(_items[index]) == null);
Debug.Assert(Old(_size) == _size - 1);
Debug.Assert(Old(_version) == _version);
for (int i = 0; i <  _size; i++)
{
    if (i < index)
        Assert(_items[i] == Old(_items[i]));
    else if (i > index)
        Assert(_items[i] == Old(_items[i-1]));
}
